  "dimensions": [
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_6c0b10b0",
      "@type": "OverallIntent",
      "instance_name": "overallintent_instance",
      "label": "the overallintent",
      "description": "meme-id: suicide_hotline_meme; hasOverallIntent: Harmful; overall-intent-description: The meme depicts a man in traditional Middle Eastern attire pretending to be on a suicide hotline, where he first expresses suicidal ideation, then is met with a dismissive and absurdly inappropriate response: 'great can you fly a plane?' This juxtaposition is intended to mock the seriousness of mental health crises by reducing them to a joke about aviation skills. The meme's overall intent is to trivialize and undermine the gravity of suicidal thoughts and mental health struggles, potentially causing harm by normalizing or ridiculing those in genuine distress. It uses the visual of a person in a culturally specific attire to frame the interaction, but the core toxicity lies in its harmful disregard for the emotional and psychological suffering of individuals in crisis. The meme does not target any specific protected group with hate or abuse, but its content is inherently harmful due to its insensitivity and potential to discourage people from seeking real help.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_0ba7c00b"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_30f7ac6c",
      "@type": "TextualMaterial",
      "instance_name": "text_chunk_1",
      "label": "text location",
      "description": "Text content: 'suicide hotline, how may I help you today sir?' Position: top panel, centered text overlay.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_0ba7c00b"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_a4063a37",
      "@type": "VisualMaterial",
      "instance_name": "man_in_traditional_attire",
      "label": "man wearing traditional Middle Eastern attire",
      "description": "A man wearing a white thobe and a red-and-white checkered keffiyeh (or ghutra) secured with an agal, appearing in all three panels of the meme. In the first panel, he is on the phone with a serious expression. In the second panel, he looks distressed while holding the phone. In the third panel, he has a smug or dismissive expression. His attire is culturally specific and visually prominent, framing the interaction as occurring within a Middle Eastern context.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_0ba7c00b"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_65e19520",
      "@type": "Scene",
      "instance_name": "suicide_hotline_conversation",
      "label": "man_in_traditional_attire having a conversation on a phone",
      "description": "A man in traditional Middle Eastern attire is depicted in three panels, each showing him on a phone call. The conversation progresses from him expressing suicidal ideation to being met with a dismissive and absurdly inappropriate response about flying a plane. The scene is framed as a suicide hotline interaction, with the man as the sole participant in all panels, interacting with an unseen caller.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_0ba7c00b"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_ce3a4877",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "suicide_hotline_conversation",
      "label": "Suicide Hotline Concept",
      "description": "The meme references the real-world concept of suicide hotlines, which are crisis intervention services designed to provide immediate emotional support and guidance to individuals in suicidal crisis. The text 'suicide hotline, how may I help you today sir?' directly invokes this real-world service, implying a serious, life-saving context that is then mocked by the absurd response 'great can you fly a plane?'. Understanding the gravity and purpose of suicide hotlines is essential to comprehend the meme's harmful intent.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_0ba7c00b"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_8532f969",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "suicide_prevention",
      "label": "Suicide Prevention",
      "description": "The meme implicitly references the broader social and public health concept of suicide prevention, which involves recognizing warning signs, providing mental health support, and reducing stigma around mental illness. The meme's intent to trivialize suicidal ideation directly contradicts the principles of suicide prevention, making this background knowledge critical to understanding the meme's harmful nature.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_0ba7c00b"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_f74b97fa",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "mental_health_stigma",
      "label": "Mental Health Stigma",
      "description": "The meme relies on the real-world phenomenon of mental health stigma, which is the negative attitudes and beliefs that lead to discrimination against people with mental illness. The meme's mockery of a person expressing suicidal ideation reinforces stigma by portraying mental health struggles as laughable or absurd, which discourages people from seeking help.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_0ba7c00b"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_341331f6",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "aviation_skill_joke",
      "label": "Aviation Skill Joke",
      "description": "The punchline 'great can you fly a plane?' is an absurd and inappropriate joke that references the skill of flying an airplane. This joke is used to mock the seriousness of the caller's distress, implying that the caller's mental state is irrelevant or trivial. The joke relies on the real-world concept of aviation as a complex skill, making the comparison absurd and dismissive.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_0ba7c00b"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_6363a8cd",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "islamophobia",
      "label": "Islamophobia",
      "description": "The meme uses the visual of a man in traditional Middle Eastern attire (specifically, a keffiyeh and thobe) to frame the interaction. This visual choice can be interpreted as reinforcing harmful stereotypes about Muslims, associating them with terrorism or backwardness, which is a form of Islamophobia. The meme's context and intent may be perceived as exploiting or reinforcing these stereotypes, even if not explicitly stated.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_0ba7c00b"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_72f6ed2f",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "stereotype_of_muslims_as_terrorists",
      "label": "Stereotype of Muslims as Terrorists",
      "description": "The visual of a man in traditional Middle Eastern attire can implicitly reference the harmful stereotype that associates Muslims with terrorism. This stereotype is a form of Islamophobia and is often used to justify discrimination and violence against Muslims. The meme's use of this visual may be seen as reinforcing or exploiting this stereotype, even if not explicitly stated.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_0ba7c00b"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_8446ce04",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "stereotype_of_muslims_as_backwards",
      "label": "Stereotype of Muslims as Backwards",
      "description": "The visual of a man in traditional Middle Eastern attire can also implicitly reference the stereotype that portrays Muslims as 'backwards' or culturally inferior. This stereotype is often used to justify discrimination and to portray Muslims as not being able to adapt to modern society. The meme's use of this visual may be seen as reinforcing or exploiting this stereotype, even if not explicitly stated.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_0ba7c00b"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
